# Grundlage

A small, zero-dependency library for building web components with tagged template literals.

## Installation

```bash
npm install grundlage
```

> Pointing an AI coding agent at this library? See [`llms.txt`](./lib/llms.txt) — a compact, complete API reference written for agents. It also ships inside the npm package.

## Concepts

### Components as Generators

Components are defined using generator functions. This gives you explicit control over the component lifecycle: setup runs until you yield a render function, the host element is passed in as the first argument, and the return value is your cleanup.

`render(generator, options?)` returns a custom element constructor — register it with `customElements.define`.

```javascript
import { render, html } from "grundlage";

customElements.define(
	"async-component",
	render(async function* (host) {
		yield html`<p>loading</p>`;

		try {
			const data = await fetch("...");
			yield html`<p>name: ${data.name}, age: ${data.age}</p>`;
		} catch (error) {
			yield html`<p>error: ${error}</p>`;
		}

		return () => {
			console.log("cleanup");
		};
	}),
);
```

Each `yield` evaluates to the host element, so inner generators or render functions can capture it without closing over the outer parameter:

```javascript
render(function* () {
	const host = yield () => html`<p>hello</p>`;
	host.addEventListener("click", () => host.update());
});
```

Yielded inner generators and render functions also receive the host element as their first argument — handy for nested rendering without re-capturing scope.

```javascript
render(function* (host) {
	yield function* (innerHost) {
		// innerHost === host
		yield html`<p>nested</p>`;
		return () => console.log("inner cleanup");
	};
});
```

### Templating

The `html` tagged template literal parses your markup once and caches the result. Subsequent renders only update the dynamic parts.

```javascript
html`<div class="card ${isActive ? "active" : ""}">
	<h2>${title}</h2>
	<p>${description}</p>
</div>`;
```

### props

Managing props on web-components is tedious. As attributes they are always strings and are not that ergonomic to get to. Other types can be saved as
properties on the element itself, but requires different handling. As a helper, we have a props function that takes an element and an object of Keys and Constructors.
They will be used for typing and validating the input regardless of attribute or property.
If the value is missing, a fallback can be provided.

```javascript
render(function* (host) {
	const result = props(host, {
		label: String,
		count: Number,
		disabled: Boolean,
		callback: Function,
		items: Array,
		missing: [String, "default"],
	});
});
```

Supported bindings:

- Content: `<p>${text}</p>`
- Attributes: `<div class=${className}>`
- Mixed attributes: `<div class="static ${dynamic}">`
- Event handlers: `<button onClick=${handler}>`
- Lists: `<ul>${items.map(i => html`<li>${i}</li>`)}</ul>`
- Nested templates: `<div>${showDetails ? html`<details>...</details>` : null}</div>`
- Dynamic tags: `<${tagName}>content</${tagName}>`
- Raw content (style/script/textarea): `<style>${css}</style>`

### Host attributes

When the outermost element of a component's render output is a `<template>`, its attributes are applied to the host element itself and the wrapper is unwrapped — its children become the shadow DOM. This is how a component sets its own `class`, `id`, `role`, or event handlers from the inside.

```javascript
render(function* (host) {
	yield () => html`
		<template class="card" role="${role}" on-form-reset=${reset}>
			<p>content</p>
		</template>
	`;
});
```

- Static, dynamic, boolean, and multi-part attributes all work and update between renders.
- Spread an object to fan out many attributes at once: `<template ${attributes}>`.
- Event handlers bind here too (e.g. the `on-form-*` handlers above).
- Attributes from a previous render are removed when the next render drops them.
- The `<template>` must be the top-level element — nesting one inside content throws.

### Reactivity

Grundlage uses a hash-based change detection system. When you call `host.update()` on the host element, it compares hashes of each expression to determine what actually changed.

This means:

- Primitives are compared by value
- Arrays and objects are compared by content (shallow)
- Nested templates are compared by structure and values
- DOM updates only happen for expressions that changed

```javascript
render(function* (host) {
	const items = [];

	const add = (text) => {
		items.push({ id: Date.now(), text });
		host.update();
	};

	yield () => html`
		<ul>
			${items.map((item) => html`<li>${item.text}</li>`)}
		</ul>
	`;
});
```

### Data loading

`load(host, fetcher, options?)` fetches what a component needs before its first render. During SSR the fetcher runs and its result is serialized into the markup; on the client that result is replayed once during hydration, then the fetcher runs as normal. A string in place of options is shorthand for `{ key }`.

```javascript
render(async function* (host) {
	const user = await load(host, () => fetch("/api/user").then((r) => r.json()));
	yield html`<p>${user.name}</p>`;
});
```

Options: `key` names the load so a keyed read ignores unkeyed scripts (survives refactors); `skipSsr` always calls the fetcher and ignores any replay.

### Form-associated components

Pass `{ formAssociated: true }` so the component participates in forms. The host gains `internals` — the `ElementInternals` from `attachInternals()` (`null` on the server). The four form lifecycle callbacks are re-dispatched as events on the host: `form-associated`, `form-disabled`, `form-reset`, `form-state-restore`. Listen imperatively with `host.addEventListener`, or declaratively via the matching `on-form-reset=${handler}` host attribute.

```javascript
customElements.define(
	"text-field",
	render(
		function* (host) {
			host.internals.setFormValue("");
			host.addEventListener("form-reset", () =>
				host.internals.setFormValue(""),
			);
			yield () =>
				html`<input
					onInput=${(event) => host.internals.setFormValue(event.target.value)}
				/>`;
		},
		{ formAssociated: true },
	),
);
```
